        "mondayCard": "On Monday: a voice you recognise is not proof of who is calling, and neither is an email that backs it up. If a call or voicemail asks for money, secrecy or a skipped step, ring the person back on a number you already hold, then report it the way your organisation asks you to.",
        "defendOnly": "You went straight to the phone and inbox this time. A familiar voice feels like proof, and these messages were made to use that. People who fall for calls like these are not stupid. Each card shows what gave the fake ones away, why the real ones were fine, and why ringing back on a number you already hold works whoever is speaking.",
        "defendIntro": "Go through the voicemails and emails and decide what to do with each one. Read each voicemail's transcript: what it asks you to do matters more than whose voice it is. Take your time: there is no trick to it, just the habits that catch these.",
